WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.759
If you're spawning subagents and getting back results you can't use,

2
00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:07.360
the problem is almost always in the design, not the execution.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.319
In this lesson, we'll look at the three design questions every subagent needs.

4
00:00:12.319 --> 00:00:14.159
Context window allocation.

5
00:00:14.159 --> 00:00:17.200
The built-in subagents you get out of the box.

6
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.959
Structured output design.

7
00:00:18.959 --> 00:00:19.920
Error reporting.

8
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.360
And the anti-patterns that make delegation counterproductive.

9
00:00:24.479 --> 00:00:28.959
Most disappointing subagent results trace back to a design decision, not a bug.

10
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.959
The three design questions are what task, what context, and what output format.

11
00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:40.240
And every effective subagent gets all of them answered before anything is spawned.

12
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.000
Structured output means the subagent returns parsable,

13
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.080
well-formed results, rather than a natural language summary.

14
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.599
Because the parent needs to process results, not interpret them.

15
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.240
Delegation overhead is the real cost of spawning and managing a subagent.

16
00:00:56.799 --> 00:01:01.520
Context loading, coordination, latency, and cost all count.

17
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.879
And for a simple task that overhead can exceed the benefit.

18
00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.519
Knowing where that line sits is the design skill this lesson builds.

19
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.040
Before spawning any subagent, answer three questions. What task?

20
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.519
Precisely what should this subagent do?

21
00:01:17.519 --> 00:01:21.279
And what does done actually look like? What context?

22
00:01:21.279 --> 00:01:24.800
Exactly what information does the subagent need to complete that task?

23
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:26.879
Not what might be useful.

24
00:01:27.599 --> 00:01:29.279
Not everything the parent knows.

25
00:01:29.839 --> 00:01:32.639
Exactly what this specific task requires.

26
00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:34.480
And what output format?

27
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.639
How should the results be structured so the parent can actually use them?

28
00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:44.239
If you skip any of the three, you've built an underspecified subagent. It might run.

29
00:01:44.239 --> 00:01:45.680
It'll return something.

30
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.080
But the results will be inconsistent, hard to process, or simply wrong

31
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.800
because the design was incomplete to begin with.

32
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.400
These three questions are the minimum viable design checklist.

33
00:01:57.599 --> 00:02:01.919
Context window space in a subagent is a finite resource.

34
00:02:01.919 --> 00:02:03.760
The same as anywhere else.

35
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.720
The difference is that the subagent's context is entirely provided by the parent.

36
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.839
There's no background knowledge being drawn from a shared session.

37
00:02:12.479 --> 00:02:14.880
What you pass is what the subagent has.

38
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.360
More context isn't better if it's not relevant to the task.

39
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.839
Overstuffing the context window with everything the parent knows

40
00:02:23.839 --> 00:02:29.199
crowds out the task-relevant content and makes the subagent less focused.

41
00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:31.839
The parent holds the full context.

42
00:02:31.839 --> 00:02:36.000
The subagent needs a focused, purposeful slice of it.

43
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.919
Disciplined context allocation keeps the subagent efficient, keeps costs lower,

44
00:02:41.919 --> 00:02:47.360
and produces better results because the subagent isn't filtering through irrelevant noise.

45
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.320
Before you hand-build every subagent, know that Clawed Code ships with a few ready-made ones.

46
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.639
Explore is built to investigate.

47
00:02:56.639 --> 00:03:02.080
It reads through the codebase and reports back what it found without making edits,

48
00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.199
which is exactly what you want during the research phase.

49
00:03:05.199 --> 00:03:10.800
Plan is a read-only research agent that gathers codebase context in plan mode.

50
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.800
It doesn't draft the approach itself.

51
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.800
It hands its findings back, and the main conversation presents the plan.

52
00:03:17.759 --> 00:03:22.399
General purpose handles open-ended tasks that don't fit a narrower role.

53
00:03:23.039 --> 00:03:26.800
What ties them together is that delegation is description-driven.

54
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.839
You describe the task clearly, and that description routes the work to the right subagent.

55
00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:34.240
One more knob worth knowing.

56
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.440
You can route each subagent to its own model.

57
00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.800
Matching model capability to task cost is how you keep a multi-agent workflow

58
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.479
both effective and affordable.

59
00:03:45.279 --> 00:03:49.279
Structured output is a design requirement, not a nice-to-have.

60
00:03:49.279 --> 00:03:54.559
When you define a subagent's task, you also define exactly what it should return.

61
00:03:55.199 --> 00:04:01.759
That means specifying a schema, JSON fields, YAML structure, or named output sections.

62
00:04:01.759 --> 00:04:06.639
The subagent should return a success or failure indicator as part of that output,

63
00:04:06.639 --> 00:04:09.279
so the parent can check it programmatically.

64
00:04:09.279 --> 00:04:13.679
Results should be machine-parsable, not natural language descriptions.

65
00:04:14.559 --> 00:04:18.000
A summary written in prose might be readable by a human,

66
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.519
but it can't be reliably parsed by the orchestrating parent agent.

67
00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:27.760
Specify the output format in the task description before the subagent runs.

68
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.279
Don't leave it implicit.

69
00:04:29.279 --> 00:04:34.079
Explicit format specification is what separates a useful subagent

70
00:04:34.079 --> 00:04:36.480
from one that returns ambiguous results.

71
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.679
Error reporting design is as important as success path design.

72
00:04:41.679 --> 00:04:45.519
A subagent that hits a failure condition has two options.

73
00:04:45.519 --> 00:04:51.200
Return an explicit error indicator with enough context for the parent to understand what failed,

74
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.600
or silently return an empty result.

75
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.559
Silent failures are the worst outcome.

76
00:04:56.559 --> 00:05:01.679
The parent receives nothing useful, has no way to distinguish a successful empty result

77
00:05:01.679 --> 00:05:04.640
from a failure, and can't take corrective action.

78
00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.600
Design the error path deliberately.

79
00:05:08.239 --> 00:05:11.600
What should the subagent return when it fails?

80
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.600
What information does the parent need to diagnose or retry?

81
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.239
Build error reporting into the output schema, just like any other output field.

82
00:05:20.959 --> 00:05:26.320
The parent's workflow depends on being able to distinguish success from failure reliably.

83
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.079
The first anti-pattern is spawning a subagent for every subtask.

84
00:05:32.079 --> 00:05:33.119
It feels thorough.

85
00:05:33.119 --> 00:05:34.959
It's actually counterproductive.

86
00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:36.880
Each spawn adds latency.

87
00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.920
Each spawn carries coordination complexity.

88
00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:45.440
Each subagent the orchestrator manages adds to the overhead of running the workflow.

89
00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:48.239
If you're delegating trivial tasks to subagents,

90
00:05:48.239 --> 00:05:51.679
you're spending more time on spawning and result processing

91
00:05:51.679 --> 00:05:54.559
than the work itself would have taken in line.

92
00:05:54.559 --> 00:05:57.519
There's one more inline signal worth naming.

93
00:05:57.519 --> 00:06:02.480
If you can't define what the subagent should hand back without the parent's in-flight state,

94
00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.079
that work belongs in line too.

95
00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.720
Reserve subagents for work that genuinely benefits from isolation,

96
00:06:08.799 --> 00:06:11.920
parallelism, or a different permission set.

97
00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.920
If you can't articulate why a task needs its own session, it probably doesn't.

98
00:06:17.119 --> 00:06:22.160
The second anti-pattern is spawning a subagent without defining its output format.

99
00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.040
When the output format isn't specified, the subagent returns whatever feels natural.

100
00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.920
That usually means a natural language summary.

101
00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.040
Natural language summaries can't be reliably parsed by the parent agent.

102
00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.920
They produce inconsistent results across runs.

103
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.279
They force the parent to interpret rather than process,

104
00:06:41.279 --> 00:06:44.559
which introduces errors and ambiguity into the workflow.

105
00:06:45.119 --> 00:06:46.559
The fix is simple.

106
00:06:46.559 --> 00:06:51.200
Define the output format in the task description before the subagent runs.

107
00:06:51.839 --> 00:06:53.200
Make it explicit.

108
00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:58.399
Specify the exact schema, the fields, the types, the success indicator.

109
00:06:58.399 --> 00:07:01.920
A well-specified output format transforms the subagent

110
00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.839
from a black box into a predictable, composable component.

111
00:07:06.399 --> 00:07:12.000
You can spawn a whole fleet of subagents in parallel and still end up with nothing usable

112
00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.519
because prose paragraphs are things the parent has to interpret rather than process.

113
00:07:18.079 --> 00:07:22.880
Deciding the task, the context, and the return format before you spawn

114
00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.760
is what makes results composable instead.

115
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.359
Just remember, a subagent that fails silently

116
00:07:29.359 --> 00:07:31.679
costs you more than one that never ran at all.

117
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.600
That's it for this lesson.

118
00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.480
I'll see you in the next one. Bye for now.

